@meta
id: 26-08
title: When the filter eats the file
chapter: 26
type: lesson
skills: filter-bang
par: 40
minutes: 6
vscode: undo, and a quiet resolve never to try that again
open: payments.log

@brief
An hour of the payment service's log. You want only the refund lines.

Do it in this order, deliberately, because the first command is the
lesson:

  1. Run `:%!grep REFUND`. Capitals. Look at what happens to the file.
  2. Get it back.
  3. Do it properly, and save the four refund lines.

`grep` is not vim: it does not know about `ignorecase`, and a pattern
that matches nothing is not an error, it is a successful search with no
results.

@keys
| `:%!grep refund` | keep only the matching lines |
| `u` | undo the filter, all of it, one step |
| `:%!grep -i refund` | the case-insensitive version, for later |

@hints
Run the capitalised one first and read the file. Then read the message at
the bottom of the screen, which tells you how many lines are left.
--
`grep` exits non-zero when it matches nothing, so vim stops to tell you
`shell returned 1` and waits for Enter before it will take another key.
Dismiss it, then undo: the whole filter is a single change in the undo
tree, so one `u` is enough no matter how many lines it destroyed.
--
`:%!grep REFUND` Enter, Enter again to clear the `shell returned 1`
prompt, `u`, `:%!grep refund` Enter, `:wq`.

@solution
    :%!grep REFUND<Enter> <Enter>  u  :%!grep refund<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

The second Enter is the hit-enter prompt: `grep` exits 1 when it matches
nothing, vim prints `shell returned 1`, and it will not read another key
until you acknowledge it. Par includes that keystroke.

The first command emptied the buffer. Not "failed and left things alone"
— emptied it. `grep` found nothing, wrote nothing to standard output, and
vim did what a filter always does: replaced the range with the output.
Zero lines of output means zero lines of file.

This is the one thing about filters that catches people, so be clear
about it. Vim does not check whether the command succeeded before
replacing your text. Run `:%!sort -Z` with a flag that does not exist and
`sort` prints its complaint on *standard error*, which goes to the screen,
prints nothing on standard output, and your file is gone. Misspell the
command name entirely and you get the same result with a shell error on
top.

Which is exactly why `u` matters here more than anywhere else. One
filter, one undo step, the whole range back. Type it the moment the
buffer looks wrong, before you try to work out what went wrong — the
diagnosis is easier with your text in front of you.

Two habits that make the whole business safe. Run risky filters on a
small range first and widen once it looks right. And keep `u` under your
finger: a filter is the only edit in vim where a typo can take the entire
file, and it is also, because of `u`, one of the cheapest to get wrong.

